# Registering the grader MCP in mcphub (2026-10-07)

Companion to `grading-surface-v1`. That memo built `agent/grademcp.py` as a stdio
server. This records what the NAS side actually requires, and why stdio alone is not
enough — findings from nas-goose, written to
`/volumeUSB1/usbshare/docker/data-sourcing-agent/MCPHUB-GRADEMCP-REGISTRATION-20261006.md`.

## mcphub cannot host a stdio server

mcphub spawns `stdio` servers as **child processes inside its own container**
(`serverConfigValidation.js`: "spawn `command`/`args` locally as the MCPHub
process"; `mcpService.js` uses `new StdioClientTransport({command, args})`). It has
**no docker CLI and no docker socket**, so it cannot `docker exec` into our
container either. A `python -m agent.grademcp` stdio entry would run where the
`agent` package and `/state` do not exist.

So the grading MCP is exposed over **streamable-http** instead, and mcphub
registers the **URL**. This is why `grademcp` grew `--transport streamable-http`
(the server is identical; only the wire changes) and why compose gained a
`grademcp` service on `ai_proxy` at port **8802** (no host port — only peers need
it).

## The registration, once the image is deployed

mcphub's source of truth is **PostgreSQL**, not `mcp_settings.json` (that JSON is
imported once on first start only) — so changes go through the **API**, and the
live `data` group id is `2c646542-70e2-4f6f-9664-962885b20006` (the id in the JSON
is stale). Auth: `POST /api/auth/login` → token, then header `x-auth-token`.

```bash
cd /volumeUSB1/usbshare/docker/mcphub
set -a; . ./..env; set +a
DATA_GROUP=2c646542-70e2-4f6f-9664-962885b20006
TOK=$(curl -s -X POST http://localhost:8781/api/auth/login \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d "{\"username\":\"admin\",\"password\":\"$MCPHUB_ADMIN_PASSWORD\"}" \
  | python3 -c 'import sys,json; print(json.load(sys.stdin)["token"])')

# 1) create the server
curl -s -X POST http://localhost:8781/api/servers \
  -H "x-auth-token: $TOK" -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d '{"name":"grademcp","config":{
        "type":"streamable-http",
        "url":"http://data-sourcing-agent-grademcp:8802/mcp",
        "description":"Gate grading: next_request, queue, record_grade, status, grades",
        "enabled":true,"owner":"admin"}}'

# 2) add it to the live 'data' group
curl -s -X POST http://localhost:8781/api/groups/$DATA_GROUP/servers \
  -H "x-auth-token: $TOK" -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d '{"serverName":"grademcp"}'
```

Group entry: `{"name":"grademcp","tools":"all","prompts":"all","resources":"all"}`.
Rollback: `DELETE /api/groups/$DATA_GROUP/servers/grademcp` then
`DELETE /api/servers/grademcp`.

## The blocker

The image publish step (`.buildkite/pipeline.yml`) is `if: build.branch == "main"`,
and the deployed NAS image is `main` — which has **no `grademcp.py` or `grader.py`**.
Those exist only on `feat/jev-gate`. So registration must follow:

1. **Merge PR #13** (`feat/jev-gate` → `main`) — triggers the GHCR build of `:latest`.
2. **Sync the NAS compose** (`/volumeUSB1/usbshare/docker/data-sourcing-agent/compose.yaml`,
   currently `app` only) with the repo's `grader` + `grademcp` services, then
   `docker compose up -d` (Watchtower will also pull the new `:latest`).
3. **Register** as above; verify `GET /api/servers/grademcp` and that it appears in
   the `data` group.

Until step 1 lands, registering points at nothing. No change has been made on the
NAS yet.
